<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>Applications</h1>
<p class="block-content">
An application is an instance of <tt>TApplication</tt> or its derived class. It manages modules that provide different functionalities and are loaded when needed. It provides services to end-users. It is the central place to store various parameters used in an application. In a PRADO application, the application instance is the only object that is globally accessible via <tt>Prado::getApplication()</tt> function call.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Applications are configured via <a href="?page=Configurations.AppConfig">application configurations</a>. They are usually created in entry scripts like the following,
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
require 'vendor/autoload.php';

$application = new \Prado\TApplication();
$application->run();
</com:TTextHighlighter>
where the method <tt>run()</tt> starts the application to handle user requests.
</p>

<h2>Directory Organization</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A minimal PRADO application contains two files: an entry file and a page template file. They must be organized as follows,
<img src="<%~directory.svg%>" class="figure" alt="Directory tree: wwwroot contains assets, protected, and index.php; protected contains pages and runtime; pages contains Home.page."/>
</p>
<ul id="u2" class="block-content">
<li><tt>wwwroot</tt> - Web document root or sub-directory.</li>
<li><tt>index.php</tt> - entry script of the PRADO application.</li>
<li><tt>assets</tt> - directory storing published private files. See <a href="?page=Advanced.Assets">assets</a> section.</li>
<li><tt>protected</tt> - application base path storing application data and private script files. This directory should be configured inaccessible to Web-inaccessible, or it may be located outside of Web directories.</li>
<li><tt>runtime</tt> - application runtime storage path. This directory is used by PRADO to store application runtime information, such as application state, cached data, etc.</li>
<li><tt>pages</tt> - base path storing all PRADO pages. See <a href="?page=Fundamentals.Services">services</a> section.</li>
<li><tt>Home.page</tt> - default page returned when users do not explicitly specify the page requested. This is a page template file. The file name without suffix is the page name. The page class is <tt>TPage</tt>. If there is also a class file <tt>Home.php</tt>, the page class becomes <tt>Home</tt>.</li>
</ul>

<p class="block-content">
A product PRADO application usually needs more files. It may include an application configuration file named <tt>application.xml</tt> under the application base path <tt>protected</tt>. The pages may be organized in directories, some of which may contain page configuration files named <tt>config.xml</tt>. Fore more details, please see <a href="?page=Configurations.Overview">configurations</a> section.
</p>

<h2>Application Deployment</h2>
<p class="block-content">
Deploying a PRADO application mainly involves copying directories. For example, to deploy the above minimal application to another server, follow the following steps,
</p>
<ol>
<li>Copy the content under <tt>wwwroot</tt> to a Web-accessible directory on the new server.</li>
<li>If needed, run <tt>composer install</tt> to install dependencies.</li>
<li>Remove all content under <tt>assets</tt> and <tt>runtime</tt> directories and make sure both directories are writable by the Web server process.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Application Lifecycle</h2>
<p class="block-content">
An application runs through a lifecycle of stages. Modules and services attach their
methods to the lifecycle events. When the application reaches a stage and raises the
corresponding event, the attached methods run in priority order. Modules included in
the PRADO release, such as <tt>TAuthManager</tt>, use the lifecycle events to do their
work. The <tt>run</tt> method drives the lifecycle.
</p>
<img src="<%~applifecycles.svg%>" class="figure" alt="Application lifecycle diagram. Application construction runs first. The initApplication stage raises the onConfiguration and onInitComplete events. The application then runs the request steps onBeginRequest, onLoadState, onLoadStateComplete, onAuthentication, onAuthenticationComplete, onAuthorization, onAuthorizationComplete, onPreRunService, runService, onSaveState, onSaveStateComplete, onPreFlushOutput, and flushOutput, then onEndRequest, and ends. An exception in any step raises onError, which then reaches onEndRequest. A TExitException in any step reaches onEndRequest without onError, then exits with a code."/>

<h3>Initialization</h3>
<p class="block-content">
<tt>run</tt> first initializes the application. Initialization loads and applies the
application configuration in order: path aliases and usings, application properties,
services, parameters, modules, and external configuration files. Each module is
instantiated, its properties are set, it receives the <tt>dyPreInit</tt> dynamic event,
and its <tt>init</tt> method runs.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
The application then raises <tt>onConfiguration</tt>. At this point the configuration is
fully applied and the modules are loaded, the request is not yet resolved, and no
service has started. A handler of this event registers additional services before the
request is routed.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.2.0" />
The application resolves the request and loads and initializes the corresponding
service. It then raises <tt>onInitComplete</tt>. At this point the modules are loaded,
the request is resolved, and the service is initialized. <tt>TShellApplication</tt>
attaches command-line argument processing to this event.
</p>

<h3>Request handling</h3>
<p class="block-content">
After initialization, the application runs the request steps in order, as the diagram
shows. Each step raises its event, and the attached module and service methods run. The
steps run in this order:
</p>
<ol class="block-content">
<li><tt>onBeginRequest</tt></li>
<li><tt>onLoadState</tt></li>
<li><tt>onLoadStateComplete</tt></li>
<li><tt>onAuthentication</tt></li>
<li><tt>onAuthenticationComplete</tt></li>
<li><tt>onAuthorization</tt></li>
<li><tt>onAuthorizationComplete</tt></li>
<li><tt>onPreRunService</tt></li>
<li><tt>runService</tt></li>
<li><tt>onSaveState</tt></li>
<li><tt>onSaveStateComplete</tt></li>
<li><tt>onPreFlushOutput</tt></li>
<li><tt>flushOutput</tt></li>
</ol>

<h3>Completion</h3>
<p class="block-content">
The application raises <tt>onEndRequest</tt> after the steps complete. A call to
<tt>completeRequest</tt> ends the request early, and the remaining steps are skipped.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
When an exception is raised during the lifecycle, the application raises
<tt>onError</tt>, handles the error, and then raises <tt>onEndRequest</tt>.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.0" />
Throwing a <tt>TExitException</tt> terminates the application gracefully from anywhere in
the lifecycle. The application raises <tt>onEndRequest</tt> and then exits with the exit
code carried by the exception, which <tt>getExitCode</tt> returns. A
<tt>TExitException</tt> does not raise <tt>onError</tt>. A handler leaves a
<tt>TExitException</tt> uncaught, because catching it interferes with the graceful
termination. The <a href="?page=Advanced.Utilities">Utilities</a> page describes
<tt>TExitException</tt>.
</p>

</com:TContent>
